1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,696
The Delaware Court of Chancery is the most important
court most people have never heard

2
00:00:13,716 --> 00:00:13,900
of.

3
00:00:14,470 --> 00:00:20,615
It has no juries. It does not hear murders, or
robberies, or divorces. It hears arguments

4
00:00:20,635 --> 00:00:26,573
about corporations — who owns them, who runs them,
and whether the people who run them

5
00:00:26,593 --> 00:00:32,739
have done what the law requires of them. Because most
of the largest companies in America

6
00:00:32,759 --> 00:00:38,697
are incorporated in Delaware, the Court of Chancery
decides, in practice, the rules of

7
00:00:38,717 --> 00:00:44,586
American capitalism. It sits in a courthouse in
downtown Wilmington, two miles from a

8
00:00:44,606 --> 00:00:50,821
small brick building on the south bank of the
Christina River where, five and a half years

9
00:00:50,841 --> 00:00:54,007
before, a man had woken up in a
leather chair.

10
00:00:54,576 --> 00:01:01,143
On the twelfth of May, twenty thirty-two, nine days
before Halvorsen Pike's annual meeting,

11
00:01:01,163 --> 00:01:04,689
the Court heard Ingrid
Halvorsen's last argument.

12
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:11,342
It was an application for an injunction. Halvorsen
Pike asked the Court to postpone the

13
00:01:11,362 --> 00:01:17,513
shareholder vote, and to bar Umbrella's four nominees
from standing, on two grounds. The

14
00:01:17,533 --> 00:01:23,474
first was ordinary: that Umbrella had leaked
confidential criminal files to the press

15
00:01:23,494 --> 00:01:29,576
to sway the vote, in breach of its duties as a
shareholder. The second was not ordinary

16
00:01:29,596 --> 00:01:30,066
at all.

17
00:01:31,637 --> 00:01:37,953
The second ground was a sworn affidavit, forty pages
long, from a former special agent

18
00:01:37,973 --> 00:01:44,215
of the Federal Bureau of Investigation named Owen
Carrick. It was attached as Exhibit

19
00:01:44,235 --> 00:01:50,552
Nineteen. And Exhibit Twenty was a small clear
plastic evidence bag containing fifteen

20
00:01:50,572 --> 00:01:52,394
flattened pieces of lead.

21
00:01:54,463 --> 00:01:59,932
The Chancellor of the Court was a woman named
Margaret Oduya, who had been a corporate

22
00:01:59,952 --> 00:02:05,484
litigator for twenty years and a judge for eight,
and who had seen, in that time, every

23
00:02:05,504 --> 00:02:11,356
trick that clever lawyers could play with a corporate
charter. She had read Exhibit Nineteen

24
00:02:11,376 --> 00:02:16,844
twice the night before, in her kitchen, with a glass
of wine. She had not finished the

25
00:02:16,864 --> 00:02:17,164
wine.

26
00:02:17,734 --> 00:02:23,355
She began the hearing by saying, very mildly, that
she had read the affidavit, and that

27
00:02:23,375 --> 00:02:28,997
she wished to hear from counsel for Halvorsen Pike
as to its relevance to the fiduciary

28
00:02:29,017 --> 00:02:34,510
questions before the Court, and that she would be
grateful if counsel could be brief.

29
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:41,477
Counsel for Halvorsen Pike was not brief. He had been
told, by Ingrid Halvorsen herself,

30
00:02:41,497 --> 00:02:43,593
that this was the whole case.

31
00:02:45,663 --> 00:02:51,738
The Director had come in person. He had not been
required to. He sat at Umbrella's counsel

32
00:02:51,758 --> 00:02:57,630
table in a black suit, beside Catherine and four
lawyers, with his hands folded and his

33
00:02:57,650 --> 00:03:03,793
sunglasses on, and he listened to Halvorsen Pike's
counsel explain to the Court of Chancery

34
00:03:03,813 --> 00:03:05,621
that he might not be human.

35
00:03:06,192 --> 00:03:12,253
It was, the Adolf half of the mind remarked, the
most enjoyable morning it had spent in

36
00:03:12,273 --> 00:03:13,651
either of its lives.

37
00:03:15,221 --> 00:03:20,939
Owen Carrick sat in the public gallery, in the back
row, in a suit he had bought for the

38
00:03:20,959 --> 00:03:26,547
occasion. He had not been allowed to testify — the
affidavit spoke for him. He watched

39
00:03:26,567 --> 00:03:32,090
the back of the Director's head for three hours,
and the Director did not turn around

40
00:03:32,110 --> 00:03:32,416
once.

41
00:03:32,986 --> 00:03:38,511
He did not need to. He knew exactly where Carrick
was sitting. He had known since the

42
00:03:38,531 --> 00:03:39,359
doors opened.

43
00:03:41,429 --> 00:03:47,469
In the afternoon, the Chancellor allowed a limited
examination of the Director himself,

44
00:03:47,489 --> 00:03:53,668
on the narrow question of Umbrella's role in the
newspaper leak. Halvorsen Pike's counsel

45
00:03:53,688 --> 00:03:58,822
used it — as everyone in the room had known he
would — for something else.

46
00:03:59,513 --> 00:04:04,514
Doctor Wesker. You're familiar with the video recorded
on the thirteenth of January, twenty

47
00:04:04,534 --> 00:04:08,984
thirty, outside your company's facility on the
south bank of the Christina River?

48
00:04:09,504 --> 00:04:13,836
I'm familiar with it. So, I believe, are
eighty million other people.

49
00:04:14,356 --> 00:04:19,133
Frame one thousand one hundred and fourteen of that
video shows a figure standing beside

50
00:04:19,153 --> 00:04:23,983
a fleeing intruder, forty metres from where the same
figure appeared four frames earlier.

51
00:04:24,003 --> 00:04:28,670
That's a velocity of approximately three hundred
metres per second. Doctor Wesker — is

52
00:04:28,690 --> 00:04:29,543
that figure you?

53
00:04:30,062 --> 00:04:30,703
Yes.

54
00:04:32,223 --> 00:04:35,864
There was a sound in the gallery. It
was not quite a gasp.

55
00:04:36,553 --> 00:04:39,707
Can you move at three hundred metres per
second, Doctor Wesker?

56
00:04:41,728 --> 00:04:47,347
Counsel, if I could run at the speed of sound, do you
imagine I would spend my afternoons

57
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:48,108
in Delaware?

58
00:04:49,627 --> 00:04:55,442
The gallery laughed. It was not loud. It was the
laughter of a room that has been holding

59
00:04:55,462 --> 00:05:01,080
its breath for three hours, and has just been given
permission to let it out. Even the

60
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:06,588
Chancellor's mouth moved, very slightly, at one
corner, before she called for order.

61
00:05:07,158 --> 00:05:12,756
And in that single laugh — the Director could feel
it, the way he could now feel a room

62
00:05:12,776 --> 00:05:18,246
tilt, with Boardroom Gravity, like the deck of a
ship — the whole case turned. Not on

63
00:05:18,266 --> 00:05:23,735
law. On a joke. On a room that had been asked to
believe something extraordinary, and

64
00:05:23,755 --> 00:05:29,225
had been given, instead, a charming man in a good
suit who found it all rather silly.

65
00:05:31,415 --> 00:05:34,510
Doctor Wesker, would you remove your
sunglasses for the Court?

66
00:05:36,028 --> 00:05:37,622
The room went quiet again.

67
00:05:38,192 --> 00:05:44,356
Carrick, in the back row, leaned forward. He had
written in the affidavit, in paragraph

68
00:05:44,376 --> 00:05:50,468
thirty-one, what Howard Lessing had told him in a
diner in Havre de Grace. Red. Like a

69
00:05:50,488 --> 00:05:52,031
coal. Pupils vertical.

70
00:05:53,603 --> 00:05:59,602
The Director took off his sunglasses, and folded
them, and set them on the rail of the

71
00:05:59,622 --> 00:06:05,831
witness stand, and looked at Halvorsen Pike's counsel
with a pair of pale, calm, entirely

72
00:06:05,851 --> 00:06:07,161
ordinary grey eyes.

73
00:06:09,350 --> 00:06:15,209
I have a mild photosensitivity. It's in my medical
records, which Umbrella produced to

74
00:06:15,229 --> 00:06:19,379
the Court in March. Is there anything
else you'd like to see?

75
00:06:21,899 --> 00:06:24,651
There was nothing else. Counsel sat down.

76
00:06:25,221 --> 00:06:30,698
In the back row, Owen Carrick closed his own eyes
for a long moment. He had known, of

77
00:06:30,718 --> 00:06:36,324
course. He had known the Director could do that —
could choose. Howard Lessing had seen

78
00:06:36,344 --> 00:06:42,145
the red because the Director had wanted him to see it.
A courtroom full of reporters would

79
00:06:42,165 --> 00:06:45,702
see nothing, because the Director
did not want them to.

80
00:06:46,271 --> 00:06:52,120
And the most important piece of evidence in Exhibit
Nineteen had just been disproved,

81
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:57,988
under oath, in front of the Court of Chancery, by a
man who had simply decided not to

82
00:06:58,008 --> 00:06:59,024
be what he was.

83
00:07:01,595 --> 00:07:06,972
The Chancellor's opinion came out four days
later. It was nineteen pages long.

84
00:07:07,541 --> 00:07:13,962
It found that Umbrella's conduct in relation to the
newspaper leak, while, quote, aggressive,

85
00:07:13,982 --> 00:07:20,264
did not breach any duty owed to Halvorsen Pike's
shareholders, since the information leaked

86
00:07:20,284 --> 00:07:26,151
was true, and material, and in the public interest.
It declined to postpone the vote.

87
00:07:26,171 --> 00:07:30,860
And it dealt with Exhibits Nineteen and
Twenty in a single footnote.

88
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:36,708
Footnote fourteen said that the Court had
considered the affidavit of Mister Carrick,

89
00:07:36,728 --> 00:07:42,006
and the physical exhibits accompanying it, and
found them, quote, speculative, and of

90
00:07:42,026 --> 00:07:47,304
no relevance to the fiduciary questions before the
Court. It added — and this was the

91
00:07:47,324 --> 00:07:52,975
sentence that would follow Owen Carrick for the rest
of his life — that the Court expressed

92
00:07:52,995 --> 00:07:56,840
no view on Mister Carrick's sincerity,
which it did not doubt.

93
00:07:59,029 --> 00:08:00,375
She pities him.

94
00:08:00,894 --> 00:08:03,029
Everyone pities him. That's the point.

95
00:08:05,050 --> 00:08:10,951
Halvorsen Pike's shareholders voted on the
twenty-first of May. Umbrella's four nominees

96
00:08:10,971 --> 00:08:16,873
were elected by margins of between nineteen and
twenty-six points. The index funds voted

97
00:08:16,893 --> 00:08:22,661
for them. The pension funds voted for them. Norway's
own sovereign wealth fund — which

98
00:08:22,681 --> 00:08:28,717
owned three percent of the company, and which Ingrid
Halvorsen had personally called twice

99
00:08:28,737 --> 00:08:29,862
— voted for them.

100
00:08:30,432 --> 00:08:36,279
Within six weeks, the new directors had persuaded
the board to redeem the poison pill.

101
00:08:36,299 --> 00:08:42,557
Within four months, Umbrella's exchange offer had been
accepted by the holders of eighty-one

102
00:08:42,577 --> 00:08:48,424
percent of the company's shares. On the ninth of
October, twenty thirty-two, Halvorsen

103
00:08:48,444 --> 00:08:53,473
Pike ceased to be an independent company,
after a hundred and forty years.

104
00:08:54,043 --> 00:08:59,591
The blue logo came down from the tower in Basel in
November. The red-and-white emblem

105
00:08:59,611 --> 00:09:05,552
went up in its place. A hundred and eighty thousand
people put their hands on their chests.

106
00:09:08,370 --> 00:09:11,777
Corporate conquest. Halvorsen Pike.

107
00:09:18,706 --> 00:09:20,168
Level forty-five.

108
00:09:22,155 --> 00:09:24,668
Territory of influence: Basel.

109
00:09:25,239 --> 00:09:31,050
The Adolf half of the mind read that line, on the
roof of the tower, in the first snow,

110
00:09:31,070 --> 00:09:36,748
with the Rhine going black beneath them, and said
nothing. It did not need to. It had

111
00:09:36,768 --> 00:09:42,446
marked this city on the map inside the skull years
before. It had waited. And now the

112
00:09:42,466 --> 00:09:44,322
System was writing its name.

113
00:09:46,893 --> 00:09:52,839
Ingrid Halvorsen resigned on the day the offer
closed. She did not attend the ceremony

114
00:09:52,859 --> 00:09:58,737
at which the blue logo came down. She did not shake
the Director's hand. She had been

115
00:09:58,757 --> 00:10:04,634
offered a place on Umbrella's advisory board, and a
very large sum of money to accept

116
00:10:04,654 --> 00:10:09,075
it, and she had declined both, in a
single sentence, in writing.

117
00:10:09,645 --> 00:10:15,656
She flew home to Norway the next morning, on the last
flight the Halvorsen Pike corporate

118
00:10:15,676 --> 00:10:18,908
jet would ever make before it
became Umbrella's.

119
00:10:19,479 --> 00:10:25,355
Somewhere over the North Sea, she opened her laptop.
There was an email waiting from a

120
00:10:25,375 --> 00:10:31,184
man in Chester, Pennsylvania. It had arrived an
hour before. It was three words long.

121
00:10:31,754 --> 00:10:33,000
Where do we start?

122
00:10:35,070 --> 00:10:40,452
She looked at it for a long time. Then she typed her
answer, and sent it, and closed the

123
00:10:40,472 --> 00:10:45,671
laptop, and looked out of the window at the black
sea until the lights of Bergen came

124
00:10:45,691 --> 00:10:46,838
up out of the dark.

125
00:10:47,408 --> 00:10:49,145
Her answer was one word.

126
00:10:50,835 --> 00:10:52,173
Basel.

127
00:10:54,692 --> 00:11:00,139
She had spent sixteen years running a company half of
whose history was written in Swiss

128
00:11:00,159 --> 00:11:05,481
records. She knew people at the cantonal archives,
and at the churches, and at the old

129
00:11:05,501 --> 00:11:10,886
private institutes that had closed in the
twenty-tens. She knew that a man who had been

130
00:11:10,906 --> 00:11:16,290
born in Basel, and schooled in Basel, and whose
mother was buried in a cemetery outside

131
00:11:16,310 --> 00:11:21,509
Basel, must have left a trace there somewhere that
could not have been manufactured.

132
00:11:22,078 --> 00:11:27,741
She was wrong about that. There was no trace. But
she was right that Basel was where to

133
00:11:27,761 --> 00:11:33,620
start — for reasons she could not yet imagine, and
that would come for her, and for Basel,

134
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:34,143
in time.
